st approval.\n \n-## Dispositions\n-\n-Drafted by the independent adjudicator in docs/reviews/wi-860-review-threat-model/002-ADJUDICATE-7f72102.md (first-approval re-sit: SR-233, LLR-311 and TC-332 returned).\n-\n-```toml\n-title = \"Settle where a review-threat-model dismissal is recorded, carry it into PROCESS.md §6, and pin it in TC-332\"\n-workstream = \"process\"\n-buildtier = \"medium\"\n-sr_refs = [\"SR-233\"]\n-priority = 8\n-```\n-\n-The re-sit accepts the rework's other three answers: SR-233's Coincident cell is gone, LLR-311 names the bold-lead home, and TC-332 pins the paragraph's content and all four examples. What remains is the new dismissal-record clause:\n-\n-1. SR-233 AcceptanceCriteria and LLR-311 Detail bind the dismissal to \"the lane's delegated-decisions record\". PROCESS_OPTIONS.md \"Delegated decisions record\" applies only to a run acting on the owner's behalf, under a dial that ships `\"off\"`. So the clause cannot be met in an attended review, or in an adopter on the shipped default. Scope it: name the delegated-decisions record where that record applies, and a home that always exists otherwise (for example, the response to the review verdict). Or state the recording obligation without a home the shipped default lacks. Keep the ruling party named.\n-2. Whatever (1) settles on, put it in the §6 \"Review threat model\" paragraph itself (PROCESS.md, plus a RESYNC_PACK note), so that LLR-311's Detail describes text the module actually carries.\n-3. TC-332 and its test: pin the recording clause from (2) in the paragraph. For \"every delivered brief that judges a review finding\", either assert the citation in each such brief, or state in Method and Expected that the reviewer brief is the only one today and how a later one (WI-865's) is held to it.\n-4. Bring SR-233, LLR-311 and TC-332 back for first approval in one sitting.\n+The first-approval re-sit (`002-ADJUDICATE-7f72102.md`) accepted those answers and returned all three rows on one claus